<CaseStudy
  title="Spreefeld"
  subtitle="Three houses, and a riverbank you can still walk along."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mitte%20Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg-001.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Spreefeld hero — the three buildings from the river (photo needed)"
  heroCredit="Fridolin freudenfett · CC BY-SA 4.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mitte_Wilhelmine-Gemberg-Weg-001.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/kalkbreite/', label: 'Kalkbreite, Zürich' }}
  next={{ href: '/r50/', label: 'R50, Berlin' }}
>
  <section class="case-reading mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-6 pt-12 sm:px-12">
    <p>Spreefeld is a cooperative ensemble of three timber-and-concrete buildings on Berlin’s Spree. The collaborating architects—Carpaneto, fatkoehl and BAR—describe a mix of conventional homes and large cluster apartments, with daycare and adaptable “option rooms” at ground level. <a class="source-link" href="https://fatkoehl.com/wohnenmixed-use/spreefeld-berlin/">[Project source]</a></p>
    <p>The published design deliberately keeps routes to and along the river accessible. This is an open arrangement of buildings, rather than a sealed perimeter block. Cluster homes combine individual space with shared domestic rooms; they are one option within the project, not a requirement that everybody live the same way.</p>
    <h2>Leave an invitation between the buildings.</h2>
    <p>The gaps are doing work. They can connect a neighborhood to an amenity instead of turning an attractive waterfront into a residents-only reward. But an open route also needs clarity: where does public access end, who cleans it, and how are noise and evening use handled?</p>
    <p>Borrow the mix of household arrangements before borrowing the silhouette. Small private units with shared kitchens suit some people; others need a fully independent apartment, care access or a quieter threshold. Participation should include a real right to opt out.</p>
    <aside class="research-note mt-8">Reading lens: project publications document the place and its intended design. They are not independent evaluations of affordability, energy performance or how every resident experiences life there. The questions here are our interpretation.</aside>
  </section>

